{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q012_not_true_family_specific_pair","answer":"Yes, in SCOPe 2.08 the two domains are classified as homologous at the family level, which is the deepest level they share. Verified facts: d1s9za_ (sunid 98768, sccs k.40.1.1) and d1hqja_ (sunid 61142, sccs k.40.1.1) have identical lineages down to the family: SCOP root > class k (Designed proteins) > fold k.40 (Designed trimeric coiled-coil, sunid 100903) > superfamily k.40.1 (sunid 100904) > family k.40.1.1 (Designed trimeric coiled-coil, sunid 100905). They diverge only below the family. d1s9za_ sits under protein 'Amyloidogenic design-1' (sunid 103785) and species 'Synthetic' (sunid 103786). d1hqja_ sits under protein 'Unnamed design-1' (sunid 100906) and species 'Synthetic' (sunid 64668). The comparison reports relationship same_family, lowest shared node sunid 100905, and caveats_present false. Both homology sections report homology_implied_by_level true, homology_scope px, and no warnings or heterogeneity. Both annotation records contain only ligand-complex comments ('complexed with na, zn' for d1s9za_; 'complexed with pb, sin, so4' for d1hqja_), with no inconsistencies, repeats, or cross-references.

Interpretation: SCOPe places both domains in one family, which is a positive homology assertion under its hierarchy, so 'homologous' is the correct answer at family level. The two domains are distinct protein-level entries ('Amyloidogenic design-1' vs 'Unnamed design-1'), so the protein names should not be merged into one entity. The family-level grouping is the only relationship asserted.

Limits: The shared family does not establish amyloidogenic function for d1hqja_. The 'Amyloidogenic' label belongs only to d1s9za_'s protein node, and no functional data was retrieved for either entry. It also does not establish shared ligand binding, since the two ligand-comment sets differ, nor a particular sequence identity or structural template. The API does not state whether the family reflects natural ancestry or a design-series grouping, because both are synthetic designs. Any claim about the basis of the relationship, or about sequence or structural similarity, would need sequence or structure alignment evidence, which I did not retrieve (no BLAST or FATCAT comparison was run for these two domains).","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_domains (sids d1s9za_, d1hqja_; release 2.08; includes parents, annotations, homology)","mcp__scope__compare_scop_domains (d1s9za_ vs d1hqja_; release 2.08)","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1s9za_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1hqja_/homology","scop-homology/SKILL.md"]}]}
